<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-phenylalanine biosynthesis III (cytosolic, plants)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-7432" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>|FRAME:PHE Phenylalanine| is one of the three proteinogenic aromatic amino acids that are produced only in plants and microorganisms. It is a central metabolite, and plants direct up to 30% of the carbon flux from photosynthesis towards its biosynthesis |CITS:[22554242]|. Moreover, |FRAME:PHE| is precursor of a plethora of secondary metabolites involved in plant defense (e.g. flavonoids |CITS:[11743081][22404468]|), signaling and protection (e.g. isoflavonoids |CITS:[12644640][20921162]|), reproduction (e.g. phenylpropanoids and volatiles |CITS:[20035037]|) and general plant fitness (e.g. vitamin E, plastoquinone, and suberin). The biosynthesis of |FRAME:PHE| has been known to comprise two major pathways, a plant pathway via |FRAME:CPD-659|, which has been claimed to occur exclusively in plastids (|FRAME: PWY-3462|) and the microbial pathway via |FRAME:PHENYL-PYRUVATE| (|FRAME: PHESYN|). However, this all-plastidal paradigm was challenged when a cytosolic |FRAME: EC-2.6.1.57| (|FRAME: G-17921|), was detected in |FRAME: TAX-4102| that significantly contributed to the cellular |FRAME:PHE| biosynthesis. The enzyme exhibited an unusual substrate preference for |FRAME:TYR| as amino donor and |FRAME:PHENYL-PYRUVATE| as the acceptor, forming |FRAME:PHE| and |FRAME:P-HYDROXY-PHENYLPYRUVATE|. Although PPY-AT also could utilize |FRAME:GLT| for |FRAME:PHE| formation, the catalytic efficiency of that reaction was lower. The aminotransferase also catalyzed the corresponding reverse reactions, but with considerably less activity. The new cytosolic aromatic amino acid transaminase step was discussed as mediator for the interconnection of |FRAME:TYR| catabolism and |FRAME:PHE| anabolism, adding another level of complexity to |FRAME:PHE| metabolism in plants |CITS:[24270997]|. The same activities were also carried out by an aromatic amino acid transaminase from |FRAME: TAX-3656| |CITS:[24270997]|. That enzyme had been previously reported to be involved in the formation of |FRAME:PHE|-derived volatiles |CITS:[20065117]|. The generated |FRAME:P-HYDROXY-PHENYLPYRUVATE| produced in the forward reaction with |FRAME:PHENYL-PYRUVATE| and |FRAME:TYR| has been proposed to be involved in the formation of |FRAME:HOMOGENTISATE|, which serves as a precursor for vitamin E (tocopherols and tocotrienols) biosynthesis (e.g. |FRAME:PWY-7436|) |CITS:[24270997]|. It should be noted that the first enzymatic step in the pathway, , catalyzed by |FRAME: EC-4.2.1.51|, remains to be elucidated, although the catalytic activity was described in extracts from &lt;i&gt;Petunia&lt;/i&gt; petals |CITS:[24270997]|.</Description>
